# Human-made production UI

Build interfaces that feel designed and maintained by a product team, not generated.

## Order of quality

1. Usability → 2. Clarity → 3. Product logic → 4. Accessibility → 5. Consistency → 6. Performance → 7. Visual polish

Never reverse this order.

## Never use (unless product-specific reason)

- Purple/blue gradients, mesh blobs, glassmorphism, glow borders, gradient text
- Pill-everything, card-everything, oversized heroes/headings
- Excessive shadows, whitespace, entrance animations, sparkle/AI icons
- Generic SaaS/marketing copy (“혁신”, “강력한”, “Get Started”, “Unlock…”)
- Fake stats/testimonials, decorative badges, Dribbble-concept layouts

## Before coding a screen

Answer: who, why here, primary task, primary action, scan/hide info, empty/error/overload/mobile.

Then set information hierarchy → interaction hierarchy → visual hierarchy.

## Visual system (this product)

- One accent (`--accent`), neutrals for surfaces/text/borders
- Prefer borders + spacing over shadows; shadows only on elevated surfaces that need them
- Radius by role (~6 / 10 / 14px), not identical everywhere
- Typography restrained; keep existing IBM Plex / Black Han Sans usage
- Primary / secondary / tertiary / destructive button hierarchy — specific action labels

## Components

Choose pattern by data (list, table, segmented control, drawer, inline action). Do not wrap everything in cards.

## States required for meaningful UI

Default, hover, focus, active, disabled, loading, empty, error, success, overflow, long content, mobile.

Empty/error copy must say what is missing/wrong and what to do next.

## Self-review before done

Would a real user trust this daily? Anything look AI-generated? Too many cards/gradients/pills/decorative motion? Primary action obvious? Mobile intentional?
